### Count Number of Substrings

**Problem:** Given a string containing lowercase English letters from `'a'` to `'j'`, count the total number of non-empty substrings in which at most one character occurs an odd number of times.

**Approach:**
- Use a bitmask to track the parity (odd or even frequency) of each character.
- Traverse the string character by character.
- Toggle the corresponding bit whenever a character is encountered.
- Maintain a HashMap to store the frequency of previously encountered prefix masks.
- For each prefix mask:
  - Count previous prefixes with the same mask (all character frequencies are even).
  - Check masks obtained by toggling each of the 10 bits individually (exactly one character has an odd frequency).
- Add these frequencies to the answer.
- Update the frequency of the current prefix mask in the HashMap.
- Return the total count of valid substrings.

**Time Complexity:** O(10 × n) = O(n) – For every character, we check the current mask and 10 possible bit toggles.

**Space Complexity:** O(2¹⁰) = O(1) – At most 1024 different parity masks can exist.

**Concepts Used:**
- String Traversal
- Bit Manipulation
- Bitmasking
- XOR Operations
- Prefix XOR
- HashMap
- Frequency Counting

###Solution

class Solution {
    public long countSubstrings(String s) {
        long totalValidSubstrings = 0;
        
        // Frequency array to store occurrences of each mask prefix
        // Since there are only 10 characters ('a' to 'j'), max mask value is 2^10 - 1 = 1023
        int[] maskCounts = new int[1024];
        
        // Base case: an empty prefix has a mask of 0
        maskCounts[0] = 1;
        
        int currentMask = 0;
        
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            // Update the mask for the current character
            int charIndex = s.charAt(i) - 'a';
            currentMask ^= (1 << charIndex);
            
            // Case 1: All characters appear an even number of times (currentMask ^ targetMask == 0)
            totalValidSubstrings += maskCounts[currentMask];
            
            // Case 2: Exactly one character appears an odd number of times
            // Check all 10 possible positions ('a' through 'j')
            for (int k = 0; k < 10; k++) {
                int targetMask = currentMask ^ (1 << k);
                totalValidSubstrings += maskCounts[targetMask];
            }
            
            // Record the current prefix mask state
            maskCounts[currentMask]++;
        }
        
        return totalValidSubstrings;
    }
}
